   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.2",
    "topic_title": "Change in Linear and Exponential Functions",
    "stem": "A function has the following values: x = 0, 1, 2, 3 and f(x) = 64, 32, 16, 8, respectively. Which statement correctly identifies a linear or exponential function that can match all four points and justifies the identification?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Linear, because the outputs decrease as x increases.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the decreasing-means-linear misconception. Decreasing outputs do not establish linear behavior; the consecutive differences are −32, −16, and −8, which are not constant. Fix: Compute consecutive output differences before identifying a function as linear."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Exponential, because each increase of 1 in x multiplies the output by 1/2.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The consecutive output ratios are 32/64 = 16/32 = 8/16 = 1/2. A constant output ratio over equal input intervals establishes exponential behavior."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Exponential, because each increase of 1 in x multiplies the output by 2.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is reciprocal-ratio confusion. Dividing an earlier output by the next gives 2, but the forward multiplicative factor is the next output divided by the earlier output. Fix: Divide each new output by the preceding output."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Neither linear nor exponential, because the consecutive output differences are not constant.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the differences-only trap. Nonconstant differences rule out a linear function, but they do not rule out an exponential function with constant output ratios. Fix: Check consecutive output ratios when the differences are not constant."
     }
    ]
   },
